  "worked_solution": "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13940317/assets/5e9d0e9d944cda9c97555ffdeac5aa245905f800cd99e11f5d172203bad3892d.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
